ees from banks  joining and also charging other fees per  message based on the type of message and  the length  today it secures trillions of dollars in  flows of trade and investment between  200 countries  so that brings us up to now  what do these russian sanctions really  entail  in february of 2022 after russia's  invasion of ukraine leaders from the uk  eu united states and canada announced  that selected banks in russia would be  disconnected from swift  a spokesperson for swift stated that the  move would be a collective benefit to  the global community they stated the  decision to impose sanctions were solely  in the hand of government bodies and  applicable  legislators so what will these sanctions  do exactly  according to eu commission president  ursula vunderlin cutting banks off will  stop them from conducting most of their  financial transactions worldwide and  effectively block russian exports and  imports end quote  but it's not all as it seems as royce's  reports quote getting kicked out of  swift doesn't make transactions  impossible it makes them much more  difficult and it increases transaction  costs significantly  julia friedlander senior fellow at the  atlantic council states banks can also  resort to alternative systems and even  communicate via email to send payment  instructions  bloomberg's in-house energy expert  xavier blas notes that the german  government said that the ban would only  affect russian banks that were already  sanctioned in other words it's an action  that really has no teeth it may purely  be political just to soothe populist  anger  so the swift ban wasn't as big a deal as  it was made out to be at first but the  thing that is really crippling russia at  the moment is the central bank sanctions  in conjunction with the swift ban the  united states banned transactions with  the russian central bank that means no  transactions in dollars euros pounds or  yen  currently the russian central bank has  630 billion dollars in